e which  moments were to be heightened and it was  actually a very careful thing with the  choreography it is really I want to ask  David and the PETA about the what I'll  call the curtain call it's a beautiful  scene at the end of the film where you  come out with the people that you play  Tim Carruthers has written a beautiful  book about this story but there's  probably something that transcends the  book about what you can learn  about the character you're playing when  the person is there with you David I'll  start with you about your conversations  with Robert and what it was about him  that you saw in him personally that  maybe wasn't captured in the book that  you wanted to bring to the screen well  in in meeting Robert and in watching  actually mirrors I think it's about  12-minute kind of documentary that she  had sent me a folk a spoon and a night  which is a beautiful documentary that  really captures the person of Robert and  then talking to him on the phone and  then meeting him in person to be  perfectly honest I had very real  trepidation about playing him because he  is a saint  I mean it's a and that typically  speaking is not the most interesting  thing to play on film you want someone  who has some edge to them who you know  who is human Robert and and mischievous  see oh yeah oh no and then I and then  with time with time he's he's not that  saintly but but no but that was the  thing that was initially intimidating  and you know also yes that the book  captures that but the thing that the  book doesn't capture as much and what I  got from being around him is just how  truthful the level of selflessness that  Robert has is you know the cost to  himself his wife was pregnant while we  were shooting this film they haven't had  an another child already they live in a  relatively small place and they had  another six kids living with them at the  time who just needed a place to say so  they have to stay so you know that  that's just the way it is and I just  thought that is so beautiful and when  you talk to Robert himself you know he  was orphaned at a at a young age went  through very real difficulty and I asked  him what is it that drives you what is  it that motivates you to do what you do  and he said to give them what I didn't  have  and then also that if just one of them  has and I'll never forget this you  saying this to me I remember it being  over lunch Robert if just one of them  has a greater self-esteem because of  something I've said to them didn't about  chess something I've said to them that's  the reward for me not easy to play  so-so you know but that that was the  thing about being around him and and you  know leaving this film leaving being  around him really in a very true sense  made me want to go into my life being a  better person  Robert is clapping there  lupita can I ask you about Harriet and  about the conversations that you had  with her I met Harriet about three weeks  before we started filming Robert took me  to meet her she doesn't speak English so  he was my interpreter and I sat with her  and what I found in her presence was  this she has she's so grounded and she  has a very mysterious kind of enigmatic  quality to her she's also very guarded  but very warm as well and I asked her a  lot about her youth and she came for a  very unstable background she was tossed  around from mother to father to  grandmother aunt she told me a story of  having to walk two days to find her  mother because she'd broken a door and  she was afraid of the consequences with  her stepmother and so when it came to  her children she too wanted to provide  them with something she didn't have  which was stability and togetherness you  know so she strove to keep them together  and that's something that really  resonated with me and I wanted to honor  that quality in her she was also very  practical I asked her why she first let  Fiona go to Robert and she said it was  because he could provide her with a cup  of porridge when I couldn't and Robert  himself admitted to me in her presence  how long it took for her to finally  trust him with her daughter and so that  she was just fear  mostly fiercely fiercely protective of  her children and that's something that I  deeply admired Medina did you have the  same chance to spend time with Fiona as  you're making the movie and what did you  guys talk about  um when I met Fiona my first time to  meet to meet Fiona it wasn't said she  was in school but one day she came to  set I mean she came we met and when we  met I never had any problem with her she  she just asked me how do you feel at all  I told her I feel very fine  because what I'm doing is from my head  and what I'm doing is like my story so  I'm doing the same thing I'm supposed to  do and when we met she just smiled to me  and I did the same thing to her yeah in  terms of what you want this movie to  look like and the way in which you want  to depict these neighborhoods poverty  the people who live in them what were  the important things that you kept  repeating that you kept talking about  your department heads about what you  wanted this movie to be and I guess at  the same time what you didn't want it to  be well this is a movie really that is  made from the inside as I said I have  lived I live in these places and I have  longed to make visual illusions to the  both the vibrancy that color of the  earth the greenness of the Equator runs  through my garden I mean the line of the  Equator so everything is incredibly  fertile you know in Uganda it's  remarkable that's why there's not the  poverty is not about begging bowls  because people have a tree in the ground  and they get avocados from it even where  the family lived in tabbouleh in the  film in katway there is an avocado tree  you know so it's an unusual place where  there is a lot of struggle but the  despair is mitigated you know and I  wanted so much to tell that both the  colors but also the sassiness and the  smartness of style to be smart is  a lot in Uganda you have to be whatever  if you have half an inch of water in a  basin you will still wash your hair and  look very elegant for the end of it you  know that's the spirit of the people I  live with so I really wanted to capture  that in the style and in the look and in  the color and in the flamboyance even of  it because it is also this for instance  they used clothes capital of the world  Uganda and everyone wear Harriet goes to  sell her dresses the market or we know  and everyone dresses from a we know even  middle class upper middle class people  and you my fish seller the other day had  a Pucci original Pucci dress on with a  Katanga wrap around her that's just the  style it is and I looked at her brand  and I said oh my god what you wearing I  mean that's that's what they are the  stories there but so you know it was  great to work with Shan Bobbitt who is a  real a child of Africa he's made several  films there he knows it also from within  and with miles Goodall another  cinematographer the second  cinematographer and that combination was  beautiful for this film an observational  feeling and then an immersive feeling of  two kinds of cameras but Stephanie  Carroll the production designer has done  many of my films from monsoon wedding to  Kama Sutra to many films and she just  her eye is impeccable so even though the  places are real the locations are also  real I put harriet in gaba that's my  local film fishing village where she  sells because the lake victoria is there  and you can't make a film or a story  about Kampala without the Lake Victoria  which is as big as the ocean and in the  center of the city so I chose the  locations were more than anything the  design but Stephanie's I enhanced always  the design I want to thank you guys for  showing up and I'm gonna thank you guys  for making this amazing movie

